# Response headers live in `_headers`. This file does the other half of the job.
#
# The site is published from the repository root, because that is exactly what
# lets this repo deploy with no build step. The cost is that everything in the
# root gets uploaded: the verification tools, this repo's README, the manifest's
# worked examples, and the function's own source. None of it belongs on a public
# page, and none of it is reachable now.
#
# The target is index.html because a 404 response still needs a body. The status
# code is the point: the file's contents are never served.
#
# `netlify/` is the function's directory as *published*, which is not the same
# path as its endpoint — that is `/.netlify/functions/watchlist-count`, with a
# leading dot, and it is deliberately not matched here.
#
# THE `!` IS NOT OPTIONAL. Netlify's redirects shadow: a rule never fires for a
# URL that resolves to a file the site actually has, and that applies to custom
# 404s as much as to rewrites. Every path below *does* resolve to a real file —
# that is the entire reason it is listed — so without the `!` Netlify would
# cheerfully serve tools/audit.mjs with a 200 and this file would achieve
# nothing while looking correct. Verified against Netlify's docs:
#   "the 404 pages will only be returned for nonexistent assets"
#   "...you can force the redirect to happen with either a ! appended to your
#    status code (for _redirects)"
#
# tools/audit.mjs fails when a file is added to the repository without a rule
# below, and fails again if a 404 rule here is missing its `!`, because a rule
# that cannot fire is worse than no rule at all.
#
# Syntax is the same as Netlify's: from, target, optional status.

/tools/*          /index.html   404!
/README.md        /index.html   404!
/.gitignore       /index.html   404!
/netlify/*        /index.html   404!

# The repository's own history. This one is not hypothetical: `publish = "."`
# exists so the folder can be drag-and-dropped, which is the one deploy mode
# where .git rides along — and `tools/dev-server.mjs` served /.git/config with a
# 200 until this rule was added, because it models Netlify honestly. That file
# names the remote, and packed-refs plus the loose objects hold every blob ever
# committed, including anything added and later removed.
#
# Note the caret-shaped gap this does NOT cover: /.netlify/* is deliberately
# left alone, because that is where the function endpoint lives — blocking it
# would 404 /api/watchlist. There is no .netlify directory in this repository;
# if a local `netlify dev` ever creates one, exclude it from the deploy rather
# than blocking the path the function is served from.
/.git             /index.html   404!
/.git/*           /index.html   404!

# Netlify is documented as not serving its own config file, but this does not
# rely on that: the audit proves the rule is here, so the behaviour is ours.
/netlify.toml     /index.html   404!

# Same reasoning for the control files themselves. If Netlify already withholds
# them these rules never fire; if it ever stops withholding them, they do. An
# inert rule costs nothing, an unfounded assumption costs the file.
/_headers         /index.html   404!
/_redirects       /index.html   404!
